{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1039848851#1_1878645056","relevance":2,"reason":"The NPR passage is about Jaime Escalante and quotes him saying his assignment was not just to teach mathematics but to teach discipline and responsibility. This supports his broader method of preparing students for future success, but it does not directly mention helping them envision specific futures such as college or careers.","learned":"Escalante described his teaching mission as teaching discipline and responsibility in addition to mathematics."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" students (+ discipline responsibility) (+ future success college))","reason":"Follow the newly found 'discipline and responsibility' wording while requiring future-oriented terms to find passages that more directly connect his methods to students envisioning future success."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"jaime escalante\" students (+ discipline responsibility) (+ future success college))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_29_1127376148#1_2542343955","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2139","rank":1,"snippet":"the Money, Lebowski? 2. Who Invited the NARC? 3. Is There A Manager I Can Talk To? 4. Your Deadlines Don't Count As Much As My Real Professor's, Right? 5. You Can't Handle the Truth! 6. Dude, Leave Me Alone! 7. So Tired…So Very Tired 8. No Playing Hooky For You 9. TA Labor Laws? It Takes Ganas: Jaime Escalante's Secret to Inspired Learning It Takes Ganas: <cover>Jaime Escalante''s Secret to Inspired Learning \" , \"segment\": \"It's wonderful. It really is. It's just a great way to deepen your knowledge in your chosen discipline, to hone marketable skills for your future career, and to forge lasting and mutually beneficial relationships with professors and your fellow students </cover>alike. As a TA, you will fall somewhere between educator and student on the educational spectrum (though admittedly much closer to the latter than the former). Being a TA brings with it a ton of opportunities, both academic and professional. We urge you to learn more about these opportunities in our piece about why Being a TA is Awesome. And honestly, being a TA is great, but it can also really suck. "}}
